/*****************************************************************************
    jitJP -- THE TABLE-ARC PROBE's rung. T1: shared dispatch, forked leaves.

    TWO CLAIMS, and the second is the one that could not be made before today.

    1. THE SCALAR LEAF STILL EMITS. `+=` on a count target compiles and runs at
       run time -- fire twice, no recompile, answers differ.

    2. ⚠ THE FALLBACK LEAF DEGRADES *LOUDLY*, AND THE COUNTER MOVES. jitDegrade
       had ZERO call sites after the iterator rework, so every rung's
       `degrade count = 0` was VACUOUS -- true, but nothing could move it. A
       Buffer target now reaches a real degrade arm. THIS RUNG DELIBERATELY
       EXPECTS A NON-ZERO COUNT, which is the opposite of every other rung and
       is exactly why it proves the assertion is live rather than decorative.

    ⚠ IT IS NOT A REGRESSION THAT THIS RUNG DEGRADES. A degrade is the DESIGNED
    behaviour for an uncovered leaf -- the interpreted body runs and the fall is
    counted. Silent fall-through is what T1 makes unconstructable; a counted one
    is the product.
*****************************************************************************/
Start();
include(unitTests);
include(generate);
include(utilities);

search reset stack Grokking;
search Generating UnitTests bcOPs Utilities list;

register(JitLadder);
define
    jpIn;
    jpOut;
    jpBuf buffer;
    jitJP  code={ jpOut = 0; jpOut += jpIn; jpOut += 100; };
    jitJPd code={ jpBuf += "x"; };
    ;

jpIn  = 5;
jpOut = 0;
print "JP fire 1 setup : jpIn =" jpIn:;
testing(jitJP);
print "JP fire 1 result: jpOut =" jpOut " (want 105)":;

jpIn  = 25;
jpOut = 0;
print "JP fire 2 setup : jpIn =" jpIn " (recompile: NONE)":;
jitRefire();
print "JP fire 2 result: jpOut =" jpOut " (want 125)":;

jpIn  = 5;
jpOut = 0;
jitJP();
print "JP interpreted  : jpOut =" jpOut " (the oracle -- want 105)":;
print "JP SENTINEL":;
stop();
